{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791620702,"nonce":"fNb4QcE0AeBfOmUd2ZMgcidczL0=","message":"ImmixAllocator::new runs once: the collector is a OnceLock singleton (GC in std/src/gc.rs, built by gc_locked_init). The 266 are not allocators. malloc_history lists VM regions, and it names every region split off an mmap by the frame that made the mmap; here that is the heap reservation's, ImmixAllocator::new + 100 (HeapMemory::new, inlined). Page-protection mode splits the reservation with an mprotect per run of write-protected blocks.\n\nChecked on 3d5ab48 with the ecc98cc reproducer (a 300 MB old graph), MallocStackLogging=lite, 35 s in, regions listed under ImmixAllocator::new + 100:\n\n    ASH_GC_CARDS=0 (page protection): 243\n    default (cards):                    9\n\nSo card mode, the default since ded1b089eb67c6143f16bd37b9a55485042bb603, does not fragment the reservation. The 256 MB and 128 MB callocs under + 220 and + 192 are the object and line-size side tables, touched lazily.\n\nClosing: the premise was repeated construction, and it is one construction. If a current main still shows a dirty GC heap far above its used blocks on ashui's gallery, that is a separate measurement and wants its own issue, with vmmap's VM_ALLOCATE row beside ASH_GC_STATS.","files":null}]}